    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of \\(5!\\times 8!\\) and \\(\\frac{8!}{3!}\\), we should first express these factorials. Notice that \\(8! = 8 \\times 7 \\times 6 \\times 5!\\) and \\(\\frac{8!}{3!} = 8 \\times 7 \\times 6 \\times 5!\\).\n\nWe then find the GCD of \\(5!\\times 8!\\) and \\(8 \\times 7 \\times 6 \\times 5!\\). Taking out the common factor \\(\\left(5!\\times 8!\\right)\\), we get \\(\\left(5! \\times 8!\\right) \\times 8 \\times 7 \\times 6 \\times 5!\\). We can simplify this expression to \\(5!\\times 8!\\).\n\nNow to find the GCD of \\(5! \\times 8!\\) with the other factor, \\(3!\\), we recognize that \\(3! = 3 \\times 2!\\).\nLet's calculate \\(GCD \\left( 5! \\times 8!, 3!\\right)=GCD \\left( 3! 5! \\times 8!, 3!\\right) = 3! \\times GCD ( 5! \\times 8!, 3!)\\). To find \\(GCD ( 5! \\times 8!, 3!)\\), we list the prime factors. Subsequently, we compare them.\n\nThe prime factorization of \\(8!\\) is \\[8! = 1 \\times 2^3 \\times 3^2 \\times 5 \\times 7.\\]\nThe prime factorization of \\(5!\\) is \\[5! = 1 \\times 2^3 \\times 3^1 \\times 5^1.\\]\nThe prime factorization of \\(3!\\) is \\[3! = 2 \\times 3.\\]\n\nIt appears that \\(\\left(\\mathrm{LCM} ( 5! \\times 8!, 3!)\\right)\\) cannot be formed by multiplying numbers of the same prime exponents with the added prime factor \\(\\mathrm{LCM} (2^3\\cdot 3^2 \\cdot 5\\cdot 7, 2 \\cdot 3)\\).\n\nNext, analyze the prime numbers present in \\(5! \\times 8!\\) and \\(3!\\):\n1. \\(\\mathrm{LCM} (5! \\times 8!, 3!)\\) must contain at least three 2s, two 3s, one 5, and one 7 from \\(8!\\) and one (additional) 2 and 2s from 3!.\n2. \\(\\mathrm{LCM} (5! \\times 8!, 3!)\\) must have a minimum of one 2 and one 3 from \\(3!\\) since both \\(3!\\) is the overlap.\n\nThe LCM will contain \\(1 \\times 2^3 \\times 3^2 \\times 5^1 \\times 7 = 840\\). Since \\(840 \\times 3! = 840 \\times 6 = 5040\\), we conclude \\(\\left(\\mathrm{LCM} (5! \\times 8!, 3!)\\right) = 5040\\).\n\nTherefore, \\(\\boxed{5040}\\) is the greatest common divisor of \\(5! \\times 8!\\) and 8!.",
